00:16:48,860 --> 00:16:52,980
is kind of deceiving in its size on my computer screen.

252
00:16:53,080 --> 00:16:56,080
This machine is over 45 feet long.

253
00:16:56,180 --> 00:17:00,780
It's 15 feet wide and 10 feet high.

254
00:17:00,781 --> 00:17:04,620
One of the design criteria when we were building this machine

255
00:17:04,720 --> 00:17:08,880
was brought on to us by our customer.

256
00:17:08,980 --> 00:17:10,860
This giant machine had to be designed

257
00:17:10,960 --> 00:17:13,700
in pieces that fit through a five-foot square shaft

258
00:17:13,800 --> 00:17:16,900
for assembly underground.

259
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:20,260
I need to ensure that when I start piecing this together

260
00:17:20,360 --> 00:17:24,000
that things do fit, things do work properly,

261
00:17:24,100 --> 00:17:27,800
so that I don't get calls from the machine shop or the assembly

262
00:17:27,801 --> 00:17:31,200
floor saying, this doesn't work or this doesn't fit.

263
00:17:31,300 --> 00:17:35,000
No engineer likes that. At the Joy Mining

264
00:17:35,100 --> 00:17:38,640
Machinery Manufacturing Plant in Franklin, Pennsylvania,

265
00:17:38,740 --> 00:17:41,940
giant machine tools form, cut, and measure the enormous

266
00:17:42,040 --> 00:17:48,960
individual parts that make up the 12HM-36 continuous miner.

267
00:17:49,060 --> 00:17:51,900
And it all begins with a plate of steel.

268
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:55,000
This is really the beginning for all our parts that are cut

269
00:17:55,001 --> 00:17:57,660
cut out of steel plate or bar stock.

270
00:17:57,661 --> 00:18:00,580
It's all started right here.

271
00:18:00,581 --> 00:18:03,440
There are seven behemoth burning machines that torch

272
00:18:03,441 --> 00:18:06,820
cut each individual piece of steel.

273
00:18:06,821 --> 00:18:10,720
With a touch of a few buttons, the burning begins.

274
00:18:10,721 --> 00:18:12,820
It's a lot like a bolt of lightning,

275
00:18:12,821 --> 00:18:17,080
except we're pushing it through an eighth inch hole.

276
00:18:17,081 --> 00:18:20,820
I'm told that the temperature of the sun is 11,000 degrees,

277
00:18:20,821 --> 00:18:23,620
but we're four times that down at the cut.

278
00:18:23,621 --> 00:18:26,660
So it's a hot cut.

279
00:18:26,760 --> 00:18:30,320
And that's the finished part.

280
00:18:30,420 --> 00:18:33,660
Among the most critical units of the 12HM36

281
00:18:33,760 --> 00:18:37,960
are the electrical box encasements.

282
00:18:38,060 --> 00:18:41,080
Our machines will work underground all their life.

283
00:18:41,180 --> 00:18:42,780
And any time you're digging underground,

284
00:18:42,880 --> 00:18:46,380
you have a chance of running into a methane gas pocket.

285
00:18:46,480 --> 00:18:49,280
Nothing will keep methane gas from penetrating

286
00:18:49,380 --> 00:18:51,820
an electrical box.

287
00:18:51,821 --> 00:18:54,560
So to keep miners safe, these electrical boxes

288
00:18:54,660 --> 00:18:57,100
are machine-tooled so perfectly,

289
00:18:57,200 --> 00:18:59,960
any ignition that might occur inside the box

290
00:19:00,060 --> 00:19:01,460
won't pass into the open air.

291
00:19:05,160 --> 00:19:07,500
This is a box that just came off.

292
00:19:07,600 --> 00:19:09,900
There can be no scratches or imperfections.

293
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:13,140
Anything that could be a flame path for burning methane gas

294
00:19:13,240 --> 00:19:16,640
can't pass between here and the cover.

295
00:19:16,740 --> 00:19:18,620
In addition to visual inspections,

296
00:19:18,720 --> 00:19:21,340
another giant machine aids in the precision measuring

297
00:19:21,341 --> 00:19:24,840
piece of the puzzle. The fact that this machine

298
00:19:24,841 --> 00:19:28,740
will measure with two tenths of a thousandth of an inch is so

299
00:19:28,741 --> 00:19:33,460
important to us because a lot of our tolerances that we have to

300
00:19:33,461 --> 00:19:37,260
hold are only one thousandth of an inch.

301
00:19:37,261 --> 00:19:40,020
Before the final pieces of the machine are sent to the

302
00:19:40,021 --> 00:19:46,940
customer, each 12 HM36 is assembled, tested, and then

303
00:19:46,941 --> 00:19:51,340
disassembled for transport.

304
00:19:51,341 --> 00:19:54,180
The machine has to be broken down into a very small size.

305
00:19:54,181 --> 00:19:57,040
It has to fit into a 5-foot square hole.

306
00:19:57,041 --> 00:20:00,080
So we're taking a 300,000-pound machine

307
00:20:00,081 --> 00:20:03,320
and breaking it into about 30 large pieces

308
00:20:03,321 --> 00:20:05,780
to put into the mine.

309
00:20:05,781 --> 00:20:08,960
The continuous miner is a machine on a grand scale,

310
00:20:08,961 --> 00:20:10,520
compared to the backbreaking tools

311
00:20:10,521 --> 00:20:13,960
of the early mining industry.

312
00:20:13,961 --> 00:20:16,060
Throughout the long history of mining,

313
00:20:16,061 --> 00:20:18,360
machines and contraptions of various sizes

314
00:20:18,361 --> 00:20:23,360
devised to move Earth. But it wasn't until the late 19th

315
00:20:23,361 --> 00:20:28,000
century that real changes began to occur, and mining could delve

316
00:20:28,001 --> 00:20:33,380
deeper under the surface. With steam power and metal

317
00:20:33,381 --> 00:20:39,380
digging tools, mining got faster and just a little easier.

318
00:20:39,381 --> 00:20:42,380
But the inherent dangers to hundreds of miners working

319
00:20:42,381 --> 00:20:46,880
hundreds of feet underground were still very real and often

320
00:20:46,881 --> 00:20:53,220
deadly. Mining now depends on machines

321
00:20:53,320 --> 00:20:56,660
like the continuous miner to do the work previously done by so

322
00:20:56,760 --> 00:21:02,220
many men. The rotating drum head,

323
00:21:02,320 --> 00:21:05,860
peppered with carbide steel teeth, is the lethal force that

324
00:21:05,960 --> 00:21:09,040
disintegrates the earth. The cutting drums on the

325
00:21:09,140 --> 00:21:13,800
machine are 15 feet wide, and they are 5 feet in diameter.

326
00:21:13,801 --> 00:21:18,800
Each drum weighs about five tons.

327
00:21:18,900 --> 00:21:20,800
The stuff of nightmares.

328
00:21:20,900 --> 00:21:24,340
This machine churns 900 feet below the surface

329
00:21:24,440 --> 00:21:27,440
and uses a conveyor system to move the displaced minerals

330
00:21:27,540 --> 00:21:30,040
to a hauler.

331
00:21:30,140 --> 00:21:31,860
All aspects of the continuous miner

332
00:21:31,960 --> 00:21:36,360
are monitored with real-time accuracy inside the mine,

333
00:21:36,460 --> 00:21:41,160
up at the surface, and anywhere on the globe.

334
00:21:41,900 --> 00:21:52,157
Being able to remotely operate or remotely diagnose from any location gives the operator in the mine the expertise of not only other people within his organization

335
00:21:52,158 --> 00:22:02,540
that are experts, but it opens them up to the Joy organization where we have a complete series of global experts that understand this equipment in its finest detail.

336
00:22:02,541 --> 00:22:06,340
This particular screen shows three separate machines

337
00:22:06,440 --> 00:22:09,940
that we've logged into in a given mine operating.

338
00:22:10,040 --> 00:22:13,280
And you can see the top view and the side view.

339
00:22:13,380 --> 00:22:15,780
And when you're actually cutting and operating,

340
00:22:15,880 --> 00:22:17,980
you'll have the slider bar on the side that

341
00:22:18,080 --> 00:22:20,200
gives us the actual mining height

342
00:22:20,300 --> 00:22:23,200
of the particular equipment.

343
00:22:23,300 --> 00:22:25,300
The massive continuous mining machine

344
00:22:25,400 --> 00:22:29,740
that revolutionized the industry will continue to get bigger.

345
00:22:29,741 --> 00:22:33,040
It is evolving all the time with new components,

346
00:22:33,140 --> 00:22:34,240
new technology.

347
00:22:34,340 --> 00:22:36,240
It's like the rest of the world.

348
00:22:36,340 --> 00:22:38,740
Technology is expanding every day.

349
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:42,180
And to meet market demands, you have to expand right along

350
00:22:42,280 --> 00:22:43,380
with it.

351
00:22:43,480 --> 00:22:45,980
These giant machines have proven their importance

352
00:22:46,080 --> 00:22:50,100
to industry in their subterranean world.

353
00:22:50,200 --> 00:22:53,100
But can a machine become the latest and greatest city

354
00:22:53,200 --> 00:22:54,100
landmark?

355
00:22:54,101 --> 00:23:04,340
The 12-HM36 continuous miner weighs more than 100 compact

356
00:23:04,341 --> 00:23:06,440
passenger cars.

357
00:23:06,441 --> 00:23:10,500
World's Biggest Machines will return on Modern Marvels here

358
00:23:10,501 --> 00:23:11,400
on the History Channel.

359
00:23:17,100 --> 00:23:19,840
We now return to World's Biggest Machines

360
00:23:19,841 --> 00:23:20,900
on Modern Marvels.

361
00:23:24,100 --> 00:23:31,840
The Eiffel Tower, the Empire State Building, Taipei 101.

362
00:23:31,940 --> 00:23:34,940
These structures are prominent landmarks that promote tourism

363
00:23:35,040 --> 00:23:38,140
and business, and display a distinctive architectural

364
00:23:38,240 --> 00:23:39,140
panache.

365
00:23:49,760 --> 00:23:52,300
But cities that want new and distinct monuments

366
00:23:52,301 --> 00:23:55,540
outside the box for alternatives.

367
00:23:55,640 --> 00:23:57,840
Why not a machine?

368
00:23:57,940 --> 00:24:00,940
Like the giant machine in the heart of London.

369
00:24:01,040 --> 00:24:02,940
I'm going to London unless you go into London Eye.

370
00:24:03,040 --> 00:24:06,940
And where else can you get the view of a London?

371
00:24:07,040 --> 00:24:09,020
It stands almost three times the height

372
00:24:09,120 --> 00:24:11,060
of the Statue of Liberty.

373
00:24:11,160 --> 00:24:14,960
It's as familiar as it is extraordinary.

374
00:24:15,060 --> 00:24:17,400
We weren't necessarily wanting the biggest,

375
00:24:17,500 --> 00:24:21,400
but it, you know, you can't resist, can you?

376
00:24:21,401 --> 00:24:23,400
I'm going to do this, can you?

377
00:24:51,400 --> 00:25:00,700
passengers more than 1,500 per day. The eye obviously resembles a

378
00:25:00,701 --> 00:25:04,300
traditional Ferris wheel, but technically the eye is different

379
00:25:04,301 --> 00:25:12,800
in three ways. First, the capsules are enclosed.

380
00:25:12,801 --> 00:25:18,600
Second, they're positioned on the outside of the wheel.

381
00:25:18,601 --> 00:25:22,100
And third, the whole structure is supported by an A-frame,

382
00:25:22,200 --> 00:25:23,600
from one side only.

383
00:25:29,340 --> 00:25:32,040
This giant wheel was originally designed and built

384
00:25:32,140 --> 00:25:35,260
for celebrations of the millennium.

385
00:25:35,360 --> 00:25:40,060
Since then, it's become London's most popular attraction.

386
00:25:40,160 --> 00:25:42,960
We decided that it shouldn't be just a landmark that you look

387
00:25:43,060 --> 00:25:44,060
at.

388
00:25:44,160 --> 00:25:48,260
It should be something that people could participate in.

389
00:25:48,261 --> 00:25:51,760
The 32 totally enclosed air-conditioned capsules are

390
00:25:51,761 --> 00:25:56,500
the most important elements to the success of the machine.

391
00:25:56,501 --> 00:25:59,300
Putting the capsules on the outside was also because we

392
00:25:59,301 --> 00:26:02,380
wanted people to have an unimpeded view through 360

393
00:26:02,381 --> 00:26:05,920
degrees, you know, the idea that you're feeling like you're on

394
00:26:05,921 --> 00:26:08,680
top of a mountain but in the middle of the city.

395
00:26:08,681 --> 00:26:11,720
Traditional ferris wheels, the ferris capsules, would have

396
00:26:11,721 --> 00:26:14,920
been hanging, and so at the top they would basically be hanging

397
00:26:14,921 --> 00:26:18,460
between the structure, and your view is obviously impeded

398
00:26:18,560 --> 00:26:22,860
by the structure here, nothing gets in the way of the view.

399
00:26:22,960 --> 00:26:25,300
The capsules are attached to the external circumference

400
00:26:25,400 --> 00:26:29,000
of the wheel instead of being suspended.

401
00:26:29,100 --> 00:26:31,640
This requires more mechanical stabilization

402
00:26:31,740 --> 00:26:33,000
and technical complexity.

403
00:26:35,700 --> 00:26:38,840
Each capsule has a track on which a mounting ring rotates

404
00:26:38,940 --> 00:26:42,280
at the exact same speed the wheel rotates.

405
00:26:42,380 --> 00:26:44,380
This ensures the capsule floor is always

406
00:26:44,381 --> 00:26:49,120
is always level and is so smooth that movement can't be felt.

407
00:26:49,121 --> 00:26:51,520
That ability to keep the floor level,

408
00:26:51,521 --> 00:26:54,220
allow people to walk around, means you have to motorize

409
00:26:54,221 --> 00:26:57,720
the capsule. The earliest reference

410
00:26:57,721 --> 00:27:01,740
to a rotating wheel used for the sole purpose of pleasure dates

411
00:27:01,741 --> 00:27:05,840
to the 17th century. It was a hand crank device known

412
00:27:05,841 --> 00:27:10,680
as the ups and downs. But in 1893, George Washington

413
00:27:10,681 --> 00:27:14,320
designed the first mechanical wheel for the Chicago World's

414
00:27:14,420 --> 00:27:17,520
Fair, and it was a big one.

415
00:27:17,620 --> 00:27:23,920
His massive machine stood 250 feet tall, weighed 2,200 tons,

416
00:27:24,020 --> 00:27:29,440
and was powered by two 1,000 horsepower steam engines.

417
00:27:29,540 --> 00:27:34,840
There were 36 gondolas, each the size of a school bus.

418
00:27:34,940 --> 00:27:39,040
This giant wheel was an instant hit and was soon nicknamed the

419
00:27:39,041 --> 00:27:43,520
the Ferris wheel. The name stuck, and the big wheel

420
00:27:43,521 --> 00:27:45,920
has become a worldwide symbol of good times.

421
00:27:48,880 --> 00:27:52,160
But engineering the London Eye was much more demanding than any

422
00:27:52,161 --> 00:27:55,760
Ferris wheel. To build this machine,

423
00:27:55,761 --> 00:28:01,120
engineers had to literally reinvent the wheel itself.

424
00:28:01,121 --> 00:28:04,160
It's a cliche to say nobody can reinvent anything, and the

425
00:28:04,161 --> 00:28:06,160
cliche is can you reinvent a wheel?

426
00:28:06,161 --> 00:28:08,800
But this wheel is different to any other wheel.

427
00:28:08,900 --> 00:28:11,700
And everybody who worked on it knows that they did reinvent

428
00:28:11,800 --> 00:28:14,660
something.

429
00:28:14,760 --> 00:28:18,500
It rotates continuously at a rate of 0.6 miles per

430
00:28:18,600 --> 00:28:19,600
hour.

431
00:28:19,700 --> 00:28:22,980
A complete revolution takes 30 minutes.

432
00:28:23,080 --> 00:28:26,080
If you stopped and started the wheel all the time,

433
00:28:26,180 --> 00:28:28,420
the journey time would be quite prolonged.

434
00:28:28,520 --> 00:28:31,920
So it's a fundamental requirement of the concept

435
00:28:32,020 --> 00:28:35,120
that we have a continuous wheel rotating all the time,

436
00:28:35,121 --> 00:28:37,220
and that we can get people onto a capsule

437
00:28:37,221 --> 00:28:38,560
that's moving in front of them.

438
00:28:38,700 --> 00:28:41,640
That means that you've got to run at a speed, which is safe.

439
00:28:42,300 --> 00:28:45,260
And that safety has to cope with a whole range of visitors,

440
00:28:45,860 --> 00:28:49,120
from young, agile people to people who are quite infirm.

441
00:28:49,180 --> 00:28:50,600
This isn't a white-knuckle ride.

442
00:28:51,800 --> 00:28:52,980
Welcome back, ladies and gentlemen.

443
00:28:53,080 --> 00:28:54,580
I just want you to step up as you come off the capsule.

444
00:28:54,940 --> 00:28:55,640
Go.

445
00:28:56,180 --> 00:28:56,880
It's nice.

446
00:28:57,060 --> 00:28:57,760
It's very good.

447
00:28:59,680 --> 00:29:02,220
Designed by Marks Barfield Architects,

448
00:29:02,221 --> 00:29:05,860
the London Eye was built in only 16 months with the help of

449
00:29:05,861 --> 00:29:10,460
hundreds of people from five European countries.

450
00:29:10,461 --> 00:29:13,160
It's the only cantilevered structure of its kind in the

451
00:29:13,161 --> 00:29:14,960
world.

452
00:29:14,961 --> 00:29:16,860
We wanted it to be light in feeling,

453
00:29:16,861 --> 00:29:18,980
even though it's massive in size.

454
00:29:18,981 --> 00:29:21,380
We very quickly came to the idea of having a triangular

455
00:29:21,381 --> 00:29:24,980
truss because visually that makes it much lighter.

456
00:29:24,981 --> 00:29:29,380
Traditionally, Ferris wheels have had a square truss.

457
00:29:29,381 --> 00:29:32,220
More like a bicycle wheel than a Ferris wheel.

458
00:29:32,221 --> 00:29:35,860
The eye is comprised of 80 spokes, over three miles of

459
00:29:35,960 --> 00:29:40,860
steel cable, all connected to a central spindle.

460
00:29:40,960 --> 00:29:44,660
That spindle is integral to the wheel's operation.

461
00:29:44,760 --> 00:29:46,760
It doesn't look that big from down here,

462
00:29:46,860 --> 00:29:49,080
but if you put it on its side, it's 25 meters.

463
00:29:49,180 --> 00:29:51,980
Well, in lay terms, that's seven stories high.

464
00:29:52,080 --> 00:29:53,380
So it's a big piece of steel.

465
00:29:56,420 --> 00:30:00,780
The wheel had to be constructed horizontally on site.

466
00:30:00,781 --> 00:30:03,780
Then it was hoisted into its vertical position,

467
00:30:03,880 --> 00:30:07,320
a groundbreaking feat for a machine this large.

468
00:30:07,420 --> 00:30:09,680
In just three days, the wheel went up

469
00:30:09,780 --> 00:30:12,020
and became the fourth tallest structure in London.

470
00:30:14,820 --> 00:30:18,400
The daily operation of the Eye involves many complex systems

471
00:30:18,500 --> 00:30:21,460
and a dedicated work staff.

472
00:30:21,560 --> 00:30:23,240
This is the London Eye Control Room.

473
00:30:23,340 --> 00:30:26,080
We have a ride operator who looks after the boarding

474
00:30:26,180 --> 00:30:30,780
process and also the guest safety while they're on the wheel.

475
00:30:30,781 --> 00:30:37,500
It's his sole responsibility and he uses the systems here to monitor capsule performance and the safety of the guests.

476
00:30:37,900 --> 00:30:42,640
What we've got here is the capsule control system which is monitoring the capsule performance.

477
00:30:43,180 --> 00:30:47,840
The standard heads-up screen is this one here which gives you the location of each capsule.

478
00:30:48,320 --> 00:30:55,580
We can interrogate any capsule by clicking on it and then we go into a more detailed look at what is happening within the capsule.

479
00:30:56,220 --> 00:30:59,520
So we can monitor the floor level which are these indicators here.

480
00:30:59,521 --> 00:31:02,420
We monitor the power supply, which is this area here.

481
00:31:02,520 --> 00:31:05,700
We monitor the doors, which is this area here.

482
00:31:05,800 --> 00:31:08,200
The drive system, which is this area here.

483
00:31:08,300 --> 00:31:12,460
And the transmission and PLC status here in the middle.

484
00:31:15,600 --> 00:31:18,580
Four sets of tires powered by a hydraulic system

485
00:31:18,680 --> 00:31:23,580
rotate the giant wheel, an amazingly simple mechanism

486
00:31:23,680 --> 00:31:27,140
for a structure so large.

487
00:31:27,141 --> 00:31:30,740
Well, we're here beside one of the four wheel sets.

488
00:31:30,840 --> 00:31:32,740
We've got four tires on each wheel set,

489
00:31:32,840 --> 00:31:34,540
giving 16 in total.

490
00:31:34,640 --> 00:31:36,580
The pressure we clamp onto the rim

491
00:31:36,680 --> 00:31:39,220
is only five tons between the wheel sets,

492
00:31:39,320 --> 00:31:40,880
and this is through normal operating

493
00:31:40,980 --> 00:31:43,620
when we have 16 wheels on.

494
00:31:43,720 --> 00:31:46,260
On close inspection, a visitor might notice

495
00:31:46,360 --> 00:31:50,400
that this giant wheel isn't really round.

496
00:31:50,500 --> 00:31:54,840
The rim itself, although looks circular from a long way away,

497
00:31:54,841 --> 00:31:57,480
away is just a series of straight edges.

498
00:31:57,580 --> 00:32:00,080
And those straights are five meters long with a weld

499
00:32:00,180 --> 00:32:03,080
at either end, and then another straight.

500
00:32:03,180 --> 00:32:06,180
Easier to construct a series of straight edges,

501
00:32:06,280 --> 00:32:08,980
their perfect alignment creates the illusion of a circle.

502
00:32:11,520 --> 00:32:14,600
Initially, the London Eye was to stand for only five years

503
00:32:14,700 --> 00:32:18,400
before being moved to another location.

504
00:32:18,500 --> 00:32:21,740
It's a startling thing to say, in the middle of an historical

